1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,957
In Japan, a train has carried people

2
00:00:02,957 --> 00:00:06,242
at six hundred three kilometers an hour,

3
00:00:06,242 --> 00:00:09,117
faster than the takeoff speed of an

4
00:00:09,117 --> 00:00:09,856
airliner.

5
00:00:10,036 --> 00:00:12,020
And it did it without touching the

6
00:00:12,020 --> 00:00:12,428
ground.

7
00:00:12,608 --> 00:00:15,386
Maglev trains have no wheels on the

8
00:00:15,386 --> 00:00:16,973
rails at full speed.

9
00:00:17,153 --> 00:00:19,605
They float, held up by magnets,

10
00:00:19,605 --> 00:00:22,610
a few millimeters or a few centimeters

11
00:00:22,610 --> 00:00:23,876
above the track.

12
00:00:24,056 --> 00:00:27,793
Today, eleven parts of a floating train,

13
00:00:27,793 --> 00:00:30,035
and why each one exists.

14
00:00:30,384 --> 00:00:32,658
The idea is more than a hundred

15
00:00:32,658 --> 00:00:33,391
years old.

16
00:00:33,571 --> 00:00:37,025
Inventors in the early nineteen hundreds dreamed

17
00:00:37,025 --> 00:00:39,039
of trains lifted by magnets,

18
00:00:39,039 --> 00:00:42,060
and in the nineteen sixties and seventies,

19
00:00:42,060 --> 00:00:45,298
Germany and Japan started building real ones.

20
00:00:45,478 --> 00:00:47,010
The promise was simple.

21
00:00:47,190 --> 00:00:50,642
No wheels means no rolling friction,

22
00:00:50,642 --> 00:00:52,273
no wear on rails,

23
00:00:52,273 --> 00:00:54,574
and almost no vibration.

24
00:00:54,754 --> 00:00:56,732
The only thing slowing the train down

25
00:00:56,732 --> 00:00:57,320
is the air.

26
00:00:57,669 --> 00:01:00,522
As always, every fact comes from a

27
00:01:00,522 --> 00:01:03,207
named source, railway operators,

28
00:01:03,207 --> 00:01:07,738
engineering references, and official accident reports,

29
00:01:07,738 --> 00:01:10,255
all listed in the description.

30
00:01:10,435 --> 00:01:13,444
Let's start with the most commercial maglev

31
00:01:13,444 --> 00:01:15,543
on Earth, the one in Shanghai.

32
00:01:15,893 --> 00:01:18,436
Why does this maglev train wrap around

33
00:01:18,436 --> 00:01:20,444
its track like a hand around a

34
00:01:20,444 --> 00:01:20,712
bar?

35
00:01:20,892 --> 00:01:23,539
Because it does not push itself up.

36
00:01:23,719 --> 00:01:24,798
It pulls.

37
00:01:24,978 --> 00:01:27,585
On the German Transrapid design,

38
00:01:27,585 --> 00:01:28,970
used in Shanghai,

39
00:01:28,970 --> 00:01:31,903
the train has arms that reach around

40
00:01:31,903 --> 00:01:34,835
and under the edges of the guideway.

41
00:01:35,015 --> 00:01:37,949
Electromagnets on those arms sit under a

42
00:01:37,949 --> 00:01:40,442
steel rail on the underside of the

43
00:01:40,442 --> 00:01:42,422
track, and they attract it.

44
00:01:42,602 --> 00:01:45,614
The magnets pull upward toward the rail,

45
00:01:45,614 --> 00:01:47,421
lifting the whole train.

46
00:01:47,770 --> 00:01:51,277
This is called electromagnetic suspension.

47
00:01:51,457 --> 00:01:53,883
It works even when the train is

48
00:01:53,883 --> 00:01:56,857
standing still, so the train floats at

49
00:01:56,857 --> 00:01:58,109
the station too.

50
00:01:58,289 --> 00:02:00,792
And because the arms wrap around the

51
00:02:00,792 --> 00:02:04,059
guideway, the train physically cannot jump off.

52
00:02:04,239 --> 00:02:06,748
Other magnets on the sides keep it

53
00:02:06,748 --> 00:02:09,405
centered, so it does not rub against

54
00:02:09,405 --> 00:02:11,914
the guideway in curves or in wind.

55
00:02:12,263 --> 00:02:16,305
The Shanghai Maglev opened for commercial service

56
00:02:16,305 --> 00:02:18,037
in two thousand four,

57
00:02:18,037 --> 00:02:20,924
linking the city to Pudong airport,

58
00:02:20,924 --> 00:02:23,316
about thirty kilometers away.

59
00:02:23,496 --> 00:02:25,445
For years, it ran at up to

60
00:02:25,445 --> 00:02:28,818
four hundred thirty-one kilometers an hour in

61
00:02:28,818 --> 00:02:31,967
daily service, covering the trip in around

62
00:02:31,967 --> 00:02:33,016
eight minutes.

63
00:02:33,196 --> 00:02:35,704
It is still the most famous floating

64
00:02:35,704 --> 00:02:38,491
train that ordinary passengers can ride.

65
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:41,936
Why does the Transrapid float only about

66
00:02:41,936 --> 00:02:44,181
a centimeter above its track?

67
00:02:44,361 --> 00:02:47,507
Because magnetic attraction is unstable.

68
00:02:47,687 --> 00:02:50,303
If the magnet gets a little closer

69
00:02:50,303 --> 00:02:51,687
to the steel rail,

70
00:02:51,687 --> 00:02:53,841
the pull gets much stronger,

71
00:02:53,841 --> 00:02:55,687
and it snaps against it.

72
00:02:55,867 --> 00:02:57,777
If it moves a little away,

73
00:02:57,777 --> 00:02:59,320
the pull gets weaker,

74
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:00,789
and the train drops.

75
00:03:00,969 --> 00:03:03,489
There is no natural balance point.

76
00:03:03,669 --> 00:03:06,629
So the gap must be controlled actively,

77
00:03:06,629 --> 00:03:07,616
all the time.

78
00:03:07,965 --> 00:03:10,777
Sensors measure the gap continuously,

79
00:03:10,777 --> 00:03:13,589
and a computer adjusts the current in

80
00:03:13,589 --> 00:03:17,313
the electromagnets thousands of times per second,

81
00:03:17,313 --> 00:03:20,504
holding the gap at around ten millimeters.

82
00:03:20,684 --> 00:03:23,284
It is like balancing a broom on

83
00:03:23,284 --> 00:03:26,219
your hand, but done by electronics,

84
00:03:26,219 --> 00:03:29,322
so fast that passengers never notice.

85
00:03:29,502 --> 00:03:30,994
If the power fails,

86
00:03:30,994 --> 00:03:33,664
the train settles down onto skids,

87
00:03:33,664 --> 00:03:36,491
which act as emergency landing gear.

88
00:03:36,841 --> 00:03:38,976
Because the gap is so small,

89
00:03:38,976 --> 00:03:41,492
the guideway has to be built with

90
00:03:41,492 --> 00:03:44,541
extreme precision, often to within a few

91
00:03:44,541 --> 00:03:47,209
millimeters along its whole length.

92
00:03:47,389 --> 00:03:50,016
That is one of the reasons maglev

93
00:03:50,016 --> 00:03:51,847
lines are so expensive.

94
00:03:52,027 --> 00:03:54,976
A normal railway can be adjusted with

95
00:03:54,976 --> 00:03:55,534
stones.

96
00:03:55,714 --> 00:03:58,686
A maglev guideway must be nearly perfect

97
00:03:58,686 --> 00:03:59,800
from the start.

98
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:02,644
Why doesn't a maglev train have an

99
00:04:02,644 --> 00:04:03,157
engine?

100
00:04:03,337 --> 00:04:05,636
Because the motor is in the track.

101
00:04:05,816 --> 00:04:08,849
Imagine a normal electric motor,

102
00:04:08,849 --> 00:04:12,735
with its round stator and spinning rotor,

103
00:04:12,735 --> 00:04:15,863
cut open and unrolled into a long

104
00:04:15,863 --> 00:04:16,811
flat line.

105
00:04:16,991 --> 00:04:18,442
That is a linear motor.

106
00:04:18,622 --> 00:04:21,773
Coils along the guideway create a magnetic

107
00:04:21,773 --> 00:04:24,324
wave that travels along the track,

108
00:04:24,324 --> 00:04:27,401
and the train's magnets are dragged along

109
00:04:27,401 --> 00:04:29,276
by it, like a surfer on a

110
00:04:29,276 --> 00:04:29,652
wave.

111
00:04:30,001 --> 00:04:31,045
To go faster,

112
00:04:31,045 --> 00:04:34,258
the control center makes the wave travel

113
00:04:34,258 --> 00:04:34,820
faster.

114
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,484
To brake, it reverses the wave.

115
00:04:37,664 --> 00:04:40,462
The train is pushed and stopped by

116
00:04:40,462 --> 00:04:41,860
the track itself,

117
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:43,012
with no gears,

118
00:04:43,012 --> 00:04:45,234
no wheels, and no friction.

119
00:04:45,414 --> 00:04:48,209
That is also why maglev trains can

120
00:04:48,209 --> 00:04:52,072
climb steeper slopes and accelerate harder than

121
00:04:52,072 --> 00:04:53,634
many normal trains.

122
00:04:53,983 --> 00:04:55,212
On some designs,

123
00:04:55,212 --> 00:04:57,901
the guideway is powered only in the

124
00:04:57,901 --> 00:04:59,974
section where the train is.

125
00:05:00,154 --> 00:05:01,599
As the train moves,

126
00:05:01,599 --> 00:05:03,423
the power moves with it,

127
00:05:03,423 --> 00:05:04,868
section by section.

128
00:05:05,048 --> 00:05:06,246
It saves energy,

129
00:05:06,246 --> 00:05:09,018
and it means two trains cannot easily

130
00:05:09,018 --> 00:05:10,666
drive into each other,

131
00:05:10,666 --> 00:05:13,812
because the track decides where each train

132
00:05:13,812 --> 00:05:14,336
can go.

133
00:05:14,516 --> 00:05:16,745
The guideway is not just a road.

134
00:05:16,925 --> 00:05:18,074
It is the engine.

135
00:05:18,424 --> 00:05:21,933
Why does Japan's maglev carry magnets colder

136
00:05:21,933 --> 00:05:23,289
than outer space?

137
00:05:23,469 --> 00:05:25,576
The Japanese system,

138
00:05:25,576 --> 00:05:30,423
called SCMaglev, uses superconducting magnets.

139
00:05:30,603 --> 00:05:34,120
When certain materials are cooled to extreme

140
00:05:34,120 --> 00:05:36,438
cold, close to absolute zero,

141
00:05:36,438 --> 00:05:39,555
their electrical resistance disappears.

142
00:05:39,735 --> 00:05:42,464
A current can flow in them almost

143
00:05:42,464 --> 00:05:44,945
forever without losing energy,

144
00:05:44,945 --> 00:05:48,501
creating a magnetic field far stronger than

145
00:05:48,501 --> 00:05:51,809
a normal electromagnet of the same size.

146
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:55,368
The magnets on the train's bogies are

147
00:05:55,368 --> 00:05:57,622
cooled with liquid helium,

148
00:05:57,622 --> 00:06:02,132
around minus two hundred sixty-nine degrees Celsius,

149
00:06:02,132 --> 00:06:06,207
and kept inside insulated containers like super

150
00:06:06,207 --> 00:06:07,508
thermos flasks.

151
00:06:07,688 --> 00:06:10,492
Japan has also tested magnets that use

152
00:06:10,492 --> 00:06:14,992
other superconducting materials at less extreme temperatures,

153
00:06:14,992 --> 00:06:17,870
to make the system simpler and cheaper.

154
00:06:18,220 --> 00:06:21,584
These strong magnets allow a much bigger

155
00:06:21,584 --> 00:06:23,855
gap than the German system,

156
00:06:23,855 --> 00:06:25,789
around ten centimeters.

157
00:06:25,969 --> 00:06:29,151
That gives a large safety margin in

158
00:06:29,151 --> 00:06:32,424
earthquakes, a big concern in Japan.

159
00:06:32,604 --> 00:06:34,424
On April twenty-first,

160
00:06:34,424 --> 00:06:37,815
twenty fifteen, a seven-car L zero series

161
00:06:37,815 --> 00:06:41,537
train reached six hundred three kilometers an

162
00:06:41,537 --> 00:06:44,184
hour on the Yamanashi test line,

163
00:06:44,184 --> 00:06:46,583
the world record for a train.

164
00:06:46,933 --> 00:06:50,119
Why does Japan's floating train still have

165
00:06:50,119 --> 00:06:51,182
rubber wheels?

166
00:06:51,362 --> 00:06:53,788
Because it can only float when it

167
00:06:53,788 --> 00:06:54,891
is moving fast.

168
00:06:55,071 --> 00:06:59,065
SCMaglev uses electrodynamic suspension.

169
00:06:59,245 --> 00:07:02,142
The train's magnets passing by coils in

170
00:07:02,142 --> 00:07:05,337
the guideway walls induce currents in those

171
00:07:05,337 --> 00:07:08,903
coils, and those currents create magnetic fields

172
00:07:08,903 --> 00:07:10,611
that push the train up.

173
00:07:10,791 --> 00:07:13,252
No motion, no current,

174
00:07:13,252 --> 00:07:14,146
no lift.

175
00:07:14,326 --> 00:07:15,725
The faster it goes,

176
00:07:15,725 --> 00:07:17,345
the stronger the lift.

177
00:07:17,695 --> 00:07:19,168
So at low speed,

178
00:07:19,168 --> 00:07:22,022
the train runs on rubber tyres,

179
00:07:22,022 --> 00:07:24,324
like an airplane taxiing.

180
00:07:24,504 --> 00:07:27,533
When it passes about one hundred fifty

181
00:07:27,533 --> 00:07:29,048
kilometers an hour,

182
00:07:29,048 --> 00:07:32,237
the magnetic lift becomes strong enough,

183
00:07:32,237 --> 00:07:34,071
and the wheels retract,

184
00:07:34,071 --> 00:07:35,905
just like landing gear.

185
00:07:36,085 --> 00:07:37,775
When it slows down,

186
00:07:37,775 --> 00:07:40,888
the wheels come out again before it

187
00:07:40,888 --> 00:07:41,600
settles.

188
00:07:41,780 --> 00:07:44,139
Passengers feel a small change,

189
00:07:44,139 --> 00:07:46,574
like a plane leaving the runway.

190
00:07:46,925 --> 00:07:49,453
This is the big difference between the

191
00:07:49,453 --> 00:07:51,116
two main maglev families.

192
00:07:51,296 --> 00:07:54,223
The German system pulls itself up with

193
00:07:54,223 --> 00:07:58,459
controlled electromagnets and floats even when stopped.

194
00:07:58,639 --> 00:08:01,310
The Japanese system is pushed up by

195
00:08:01,310 --> 00:08:03,829
induced currents and needs speed.

196
00:08:04,009 --> 00:08:04,787
Both float.

197
00:08:04,967 --> 00:08:07,683
They just do it in opposite ways.

198
00:08:08,034 --> 00:08:11,029
Why does the Japanese maglev guideway have

199
00:08:11,029 --> 00:08:13,525
coils shaped like the number eight?

200
00:08:13,705 --> 00:08:15,841
They are called null flux coils,

201
00:08:15,841 --> 00:08:17,710
and they are a clever trick.

202
00:08:17,890 --> 00:08:20,311
Each coil has an upper loop and

203
00:08:20,311 --> 00:08:23,591
a lower loop wound in opposite directions.

204
00:08:23,771 --> 00:08:27,069
When the train's magnet passes exactly in

205
00:08:27,069 --> 00:08:29,965
the middle, the two loops cancel out

206
00:08:29,965 --> 00:08:31,654
and no current flows.

207
00:08:31,834 --> 00:08:34,133
No current, no drag.

208
00:08:34,482 --> 00:08:36,918
But if the train drops a little,

209
00:08:36,918 --> 00:08:39,354
the magnet sits more in front of

210
00:08:39,354 --> 00:08:40,495
the lower loop,

211
00:08:40,495 --> 00:08:41,941
the balance breaks,

212
00:08:41,941 --> 00:08:44,833
current flows, and the coil pushes the

213
00:08:44,833 --> 00:08:47,345
train back up and pulls it toward

214
00:08:47,345 --> 00:08:48,182
the center.

215
00:08:48,362 --> 00:08:51,659
The coils also connect across the guideway,

216
00:08:51,659 --> 00:08:53,883
so if the train drifts to one

217
00:08:53,883 --> 00:08:55,723
side, it is pushed back.

218
00:08:55,903 --> 00:08:58,105
The train is held in the middle

219
00:08:58,105 --> 00:09:00,663
automatically, without any computer.

220
00:09:01,014 --> 00:09:02,790
That is the beauty of the design.

221
00:09:02,970 --> 00:09:05,714
A heavy train is lifted and steered

222
00:09:05,714 --> 00:09:08,145
by coils that do nothing at all

223
00:09:08,145 --> 00:09:10,340
while everything is perfect,

224
00:09:10,340 --> 00:09:13,320
and push back only when something goes

225
00:09:13,320 --> 00:09:13,790
wrong.

226
00:09:13,970 --> 00:09:16,616
Physics does the control work,

227
00:09:16,616 --> 00:09:19,614
without sensors, without software,

228
00:09:19,614 --> 00:09:22,260
thousands of times per second.

229
00:09:22,610 --> 00:09:25,489
How does a maglev train change tracks?

230
00:09:25,669 --> 00:09:28,799
A normal railway switch moves thin rails

231
00:09:28,799 --> 00:09:30,208
a few centimeters.

232
00:09:30,388 --> 00:09:32,650
But a maglev wraps around a thick

233
00:09:32,650 --> 00:09:35,048
guideway beam, so there are no thin

234
00:09:35,048 --> 00:09:36,008
rails to move.

235
00:09:36,188 --> 00:09:38,534
The answer is a switch that bends

236
00:09:38,534 --> 00:09:39,601
the whole beam.

237
00:09:39,781 --> 00:09:41,351
On the Transrapid,

238
00:09:41,351 --> 00:09:43,794
a steel section of guideway,

239
00:09:43,794 --> 00:09:47,022
up to around one hundred fifty meters

240
00:09:47,022 --> 00:09:50,075
long, is pushed sideways by motors,

241
00:09:50,075 --> 00:09:53,390
flexing gently into a curve that leads

242
00:09:53,390 --> 00:09:55,048
to the other track.

243
00:09:55,398 --> 00:09:58,058
Bending steel that big takes time,

244
00:09:58,058 --> 00:09:59,779
often several seconds,

245
00:09:59,779 --> 00:10:02,675
and the switch must lock perfectly in

246
00:10:02,675 --> 00:10:05,022
place before a train can pass.

247
00:10:05,202 --> 00:10:07,557
For high speed straight through,

248
00:10:07,557 --> 00:10:09,103
the beam is straight.

249
00:10:09,283 --> 00:10:10,546
For the branch,

250
00:10:10,546 --> 00:10:11,303
it bends.

251
00:10:11,483 --> 00:10:14,032
It is one of the strangest sights

252
00:10:14,032 --> 00:10:15,809
in railway engineering,

253
00:10:15,809 --> 00:10:18,357
a massive track that moves like a

254
00:10:18,357 --> 00:10:18,821
snake.

255
00:10:19,171 --> 00:10:22,240
Switches are one of the reasons maglev

256
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:24,743
is hard to build into networks.

257
00:10:24,923 --> 00:10:26,992
Normal railways can branch,

258
00:10:26,992 --> 00:10:28,755
merge and cross easily,

259
00:10:28,755 --> 00:10:31,130
and trains can use old lines to

260
00:10:31,130 --> 00:10:32,586
reach city centers.

261
00:10:32,766 --> 00:10:35,814
Maglev needs its own guideway all the

262
00:10:35,814 --> 00:10:39,026
way, with no connection to the existing

263
00:10:39,026 --> 00:10:39,685
railway.

264
00:10:39,865 --> 00:10:43,423
Every station, depot and branch must be

265
00:10:43,423 --> 00:10:44,335
built new.

266
00:10:44,686 --> 00:10:47,671
Why are many maglev tracks built high

267
00:10:47,671 --> 00:10:49,527
up on concrete pillars?

268
00:10:49,707 --> 00:10:51,100
Partly for safety.

269
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:54,778
A train going four hundred kilometers an

270
00:10:54,778 --> 00:10:57,752
hour cannot share space with cars,

271
00:10:57,752 --> 00:10:59,326
people or animals.

272
00:10:59,506 --> 00:11:02,775
An elevated guideway separates the train from

273
00:11:02,775 --> 00:11:04,010
everything below.

274
00:11:04,190 --> 00:11:07,028
It also keeps the precise guideway away

275
00:11:07,028 --> 00:11:09,503
from flooding and ground movement,

276
00:11:09,503 --> 00:11:12,341
and takes less land than an embankment.

277
00:11:12,691 --> 00:11:16,072
In Germany, the Transrapid test line in

278
00:11:16,072 --> 00:11:19,194
Emsland was built mostly on pillars,

279
00:11:19,194 --> 00:11:22,489
with cows grazing in the fields below.

280
00:11:22,669 --> 00:11:25,858
Engineers tested trains there for decades,

281
00:11:25,858 --> 00:11:29,047
at speeds above four hundred kilometers an

282
00:11:29,047 --> 00:11:31,704
hour, and Germany hoped to sell the

283
00:11:31,704 --> 00:11:33,223
system to the world.

284
00:11:33,403 --> 00:11:35,272
But apart from Shanghai,

285
00:11:35,272 --> 00:11:37,141
almost nobody bought it.

286
00:11:37,492 --> 00:11:39,152
The reason was cost.

287
00:11:39,332 --> 00:11:42,075
A maglev line costs much more per

288
00:11:42,075 --> 00:11:45,068
kilometer than a high speed railway,

289
00:11:45,068 --> 00:11:48,144
and it cannot use any existing track.

290
00:11:48,324 --> 00:11:51,279
High speed trains on steel wheels kept

291
00:11:51,279 --> 00:11:55,635
getting faster, reaching three hundred twenty kilometers

292
00:11:55,635 --> 00:11:57,112
an hour in service.

293
00:11:57,292 --> 00:11:58,677
For most routes,

294
00:11:58,677 --> 00:12:01,531
the extra speed of maglev was not

295
00:12:01,531 --> 00:12:03,434
worth the extra price.

296
00:12:03,785 --> 00:12:06,718
Why will Japan's new maglev line run

297
00:12:06,718 --> 00:12:08,266
mostly underground?

298
00:12:08,446 --> 00:12:11,739
The Chuo Shinkansen is planned to link

299
00:12:11,739 --> 00:12:13,212
Tokyo and Nagoya,

300
00:12:13,212 --> 00:12:14,599
and later Osaka,

301
00:12:14,599 --> 00:12:17,632
cutting the Tokyo to Nagoya trip to

302
00:12:17,632 --> 00:12:19,452
around forty minutes.

303
00:12:19,632 --> 00:12:20,816
To go that fast,

304
00:12:20,816 --> 00:12:23,185
it needs a nearly straight path.

305
00:12:23,365 --> 00:12:26,642
And between Tokyo and Nagoya are the

306
00:12:26,642 --> 00:12:27,916
Japanese Alps.

307
00:12:28,096 --> 00:12:30,643
So the line will run through the

308
00:12:30,643 --> 00:12:32,394
mountains, in tunnels,

309
00:12:32,394 --> 00:12:34,225
for most of its length,

310
00:12:34,225 --> 00:12:36,374
including deep under Tokyo.

311
00:12:36,724 --> 00:12:38,802
Building it has been difficult.

312
00:12:38,982 --> 00:12:41,754
Tunnels under the Southern Alps are among

313
00:12:41,754 --> 00:12:43,444
the hardest in the world,

314
00:12:43,444 --> 00:12:45,270
and in Shizuoka Prefecture,

315
00:12:45,270 --> 00:12:48,042
local concerns about water flowing out of

316
00:12:48,042 --> 00:12:50,747
the river Oi through the tunnels delayed

317
00:12:50,747 --> 00:12:52,031
the work for years.

318
00:12:52,211 --> 00:12:56,223
The original opening target of twenty twenty-seven

319
00:12:56,223 --> 00:12:58,952
was abandoned, and the line is now

320
00:12:58,952 --> 00:13:01,279
expected to open years later.

321
00:13:01,629 --> 00:13:03,253
Even with the delays,

322
00:13:03,253 --> 00:13:05,420
Japan continues to build it,

323
00:13:05,420 --> 00:13:07,741
partly as a backup for the old

324
00:13:07,741 --> 00:13:11,223
Tokaido Shinkansen, which is busy and exposed

325
00:13:11,223 --> 00:13:13,622
to earthquakes along the coast.

326
00:13:13,802 --> 00:13:16,667
A second line through the mountains,

327
00:13:16,667 --> 00:13:19,930
built for six hundred kilometers an hour,

328
00:13:19,930 --> 00:13:23,830
would give Japan two independent arteries between

329
00:13:23,830 --> 00:13:25,342
its biggest cities.

330
00:13:25,692 --> 00:13:29,072
Why did Germany's own maglev test track

331
00:13:29,072 --> 00:13:29,592
close?

332
00:13:29,772 --> 00:13:31,849
On September twenty-second,

333
00:13:31,849 --> 00:13:33,157
two thousand six,

334
00:13:33,157 --> 00:13:36,080
a Transrapid train on the Emsland test

335
00:13:36,080 --> 00:13:39,387
track near Lathen hit a maintenance vehicle

336
00:13:39,387 --> 00:13:42,079
that had been left on the guideway.

337
00:13:42,259 --> 00:13:44,980
The train was traveling at around one

338
00:13:44,980 --> 00:13:47,553
hundred seventy kilometers an hour.

339
00:13:47,733 --> 00:13:49,475
Twenty-three people died.

340
00:13:49,655 --> 00:13:52,264
It was the first fatal accident in

341
00:13:52,264 --> 00:13:54,566
the history of the Transrapid.

342
00:13:54,916 --> 00:13:57,895
Investigators found that the system had no

343
00:13:57,895 --> 00:14:01,017
automatic way to detect that the maintenance

344
00:14:01,017 --> 00:14:03,217
vehicle was still on the track.

345
00:14:03,397 --> 00:14:06,098
The train was allowed to start based

346
00:14:06,098 --> 00:14:07,599
on human procedures,

347
00:14:07,599 --> 00:14:09,701
and those procedures failed.

348
00:14:09,881 --> 00:14:13,978
A floating, computer-controlled train was stopped by

349
00:14:13,978 --> 00:14:15,396
a simple question.

350
00:14:15,576 --> 00:14:16,876
Is the track clear?

351
00:14:17,226 --> 00:14:19,936
The test track closed a few years

352
00:14:19,936 --> 00:14:23,468
later, and Germany's dream of exporting the

353
00:14:23,468 --> 00:14:24,865
Transrapid faded.

354
00:14:25,045 --> 00:14:28,272
The lesson applies to every advanced machine.

355
00:14:28,452 --> 00:14:32,105
The most futuristic technology still depends on

356
00:14:32,105 --> 00:14:34,281
the most basic safety rules.

357
00:14:34,461 --> 00:14:36,411
Today, other countries,

358
00:14:36,411 --> 00:14:40,481
especially China, continue to develop new maglev

359
00:14:40,481 --> 00:14:43,194
trains, and the race to float is

360
00:14:43,194 --> 00:14:43,958
not over.

361
00:14:44,308 --> 00:14:47,874
Why do Japan's maglev cars need magnetic

362
00:14:47,874 --> 00:14:48,766
shielding?

363
00:14:48,946 --> 00:14:52,996
Because the superconducting magnets are extremely strong,

364
00:14:52,996 --> 00:14:54,914
and they sit on the bogies,

365
00:14:54,914 --> 00:14:57,259
right under the ends of the cars.

366
00:14:57,439 --> 00:15:01,302
Strong magnetic fields can affect things like

367
00:15:01,302 --> 00:15:04,822
pacemakers, credit cards and electronics.

368
00:15:05,002 --> 00:15:07,827
So the cars are designed with shielding,

369
00:15:07,827 --> 00:15:10,086
and the seats are placed so that

370
00:15:10,086 --> 00:15:13,123
passengers are kept away from the strongest

371
00:15:13,123 --> 00:15:13,617
fields.

372
00:15:13,967 --> 00:15:17,647
JR Central says the magnetic field inside

373
00:15:17,647 --> 00:15:22,585
the passenger area stays below international guidelines

374
00:15:22,585 --> 00:15:24,381
for public exposure.

375
00:15:24,561 --> 00:15:27,542
The bogies are placed between the cars,

376
00:15:27,542 --> 00:15:29,071
not under the seats,

377
00:15:29,071 --> 00:15:31,746
which keeps the magnets as far from

378
00:15:31,746 --> 00:15:33,199
people as possible.

379
00:15:33,379 --> 00:15:36,530
The boarding bridges at stations are also

380
00:15:36,530 --> 00:15:39,759
designed so passengers never walk close to

381
00:15:39,759 --> 00:15:40,681
the magnets.

382
00:15:41,032 --> 00:15:43,146
It is a reminder that a new

383
00:15:43,146 --> 00:15:45,652
technology brings new questions.

384
00:15:45,832 --> 00:15:48,374
A normal train never had to think

385
00:15:48,374 --> 00:15:51,301
about the magnetic field in its cabin.

386
00:15:51,481 --> 00:15:52,744
A maglev does,

387
00:15:52,744 --> 00:15:54,099
for every seat,

388
00:15:54,099 --> 00:15:56,717
every door and every station.

389
00:15:56,897 --> 00:15:59,305
Floating is not just about lifting a

390
00:15:59,305 --> 00:15:59,706
train.

391
00:15:59,886 --> 00:16:02,318
It is about making that train safe

392
00:16:02,318 --> 00:16:03,033
to sit in.

393
00:16:03,383 --> 00:16:05,484
So here are the eleven.

394
00:16:05,664 --> 00:16:07,835
Arms that hug the track and pull

395
00:16:07,835 --> 00:16:08,717
the train up.

396
00:16:08,897 --> 00:16:11,126
A gap of one centimeter,

397
00:16:11,126 --> 00:16:14,749
balanced thousands of times per second.

398
00:16:14,929 --> 00:16:17,193
A motor built into the track.

399
00:16:17,373 --> 00:16:19,456
Magnets colder than space,

400
00:16:19,456 --> 00:16:21,459
and wheels for low speed.

401
00:16:21,639 --> 00:16:23,689
Coils shaped like an eight,

402
00:16:23,689 --> 00:16:26,118
a track that bends like a snake,

403
00:16:26,118 --> 00:16:27,712
guideways in the air,

404
00:16:27,712 --> 00:16:29,913
a line through the mountains,

405
00:16:29,913 --> 00:16:31,507
a lesson from Lathen,

406
00:16:31,507 --> 00:16:34,012
and a cabin shielded from its own

407
00:16:34,012 --> 00:16:34,619
magnets.

408
00:16:34,970 --> 00:16:38,092
A maglev looks like science fiction,

409
00:16:38,092 --> 00:16:40,607
but every part of it solves a

410
00:16:40,607 --> 00:16:42,168
very real problem.

411
00:16:42,348 --> 00:16:45,307
If you want more machines explained part

412
00:16:45,307 --> 00:16:48,118
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

413
00:16:48,298 --> 00:16:50,354
And tell us in the comments which

414
00:16:50,354 --> 00:16:52,410
machine you want us to take apart

415
00:16:52,410 --> 00:16:52,721
next.
